parts.text:
yes this is the mcp config 
{
  "wordpress-studio": {
    "command": "studio",
    "args": [
      "mcp"
    ]
  }
} . you can also read the documentation here in the browser https://developer.wordpress.com/docs/developer-tools/studio/mcp-on-studio/

The user wants me to:
1. Create the `.opencode/mcp.json` file with the Studio MCP config
2. Fix 4 issues on the WordPress site:
   - Dia Text: text should stay white after animation (currently goes transparent)
   - Typewriter: animation doesn't work - just shows blinking cursor at last character
   - Text Glitch: doesn't animate - just transitions on hover without animation
   - Text Cycle: uses blue color for last word that updates as animations

Let me first create the MCP config file, then work on the fixes.

For the fixes, I need to understand each issue:

1. **Dia Text text color**: The animation sets `contentEl.style.color = 'transparent'` to make the background-clip:text work, then at the end clears it with `contentEl.style.color = ''`. The user wants the text to stay white after animation. I need to set it to a permanent visible color.

Looking at the current code in `frontend.js`:
```javascript
function animateSweep(callback) {
    // ...
    function frame(now) {
        // ...
        if (t < 1) {
            requestAnimationFrame(frame);
        } else {
            contentEl.style.backgroundImage = '';
            contentEl.style.color = '';
            contentEl.style.webkitBackgroundClip = '';
            contentEl.style.backgroundClip = '';
            if (callback) callback();
        }
    }
    requestAnimationFrame(frame);
}
```

After animation, it clears all styles. The user wants the text to be white after the sweep. I should set the color to white after animation finishes instead of clearing it.

2. **Typewriter not working**: The user says it just shows the blinking cursor. This means the JS is running but the typing animation isn't working properly. Let me look at the code:

```javascript
function initTypewriter() {
    document.querySelectorAll('[data-wp21-block="typewriter"]').forEach(function (el) {
      var raw = el.getAttribute('data-wp21-words');
      var words;
      try { words = JSON.parse(raw); } catch (e) { words = [{ text: 'Hello' }]; }
      if (!Array.isArray(words)) words = [{ text: 'Hello' }];

      var cursorColor = el.getAttribute('data-wp21-cursor-color') || '#3b82f6';
      var textEl = el.querySelector('.wp21-typewriter-text');
      if (!textEl) return;

      textEl.style.borderRightColor = cursorColor;

      var wordIndex = 0;
      var charIndex = 0;
      var isDeleting = false;
      var typeSpeed = 80;
      var deleteSpeed = 40;
      var pauseAfterWord = 1500;

      function type() {
        var currentWord = words[wordIndex].text || '';
        if (!isDeleting) {
          charIndex++;
          textEl.textContent = currentWord.substring(0, charIndex);
          if (charIndex === currentWord.length) {
            isDeleting = true;
            setTimeout(type, pauseAfterWord);
            return;
          }
          setTimeout(type, typeSpeed);
        } else {
          charIndex--;
          textEl.textContent = currentWord.substring(0, charIndex);
          if (charIndex === 0) {
            isDeleting = false;
            wordIndex = (wordIndex + 1) % words.length;
          }
          setTimeout(type, deleteSpeed);
        }
      }

      setTimeout(type, 1000);
    });
}
```

Looking at the typewriter HTML from the render callback:
```php
function wp21_render_typewriter($attributes) {
    // ...
    return sprintf(
        '<div %s><span class="wp21-typewriter-text" style="border-right-color:%s">%s</span></div>',
        $wrapper,
        esc_attr($cursor_color),
        trim($word_html)
    );
}
```

The initial text content is set to all words joined together. Then the typewriter starts typing... but wait, the initial text already shows all the words! The `charIndex` starts at 0 and increments, so it starts with empty string then adds characters. But the `textContent` is populated with ALL words initially from the server-rendered HTML.

When the JS runs, it starts with `charIndex = 0`, then increments to 1 and sets textContent to the first character of the first word. But because the initial textContent from the server has all words, the visual text will be:
- Initial: "Ideas become reality with 21st.dev"
- After 80ms: "I" (only first char of first word)
- After 160ms: "Id"
- etc.

This means the text starts with all words and then jumps back to just the first character! That's the flicker. The issue is that the initial HTML has all the words visible, then the JS wipes it and starts typing from scratch.

I should either:
1. Start the typewriter with all text visible (charIndex = max length) and then start deleting
2. Or hide the initial text and only show it after the JS runs

